          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"A “new name” primarily signifies a new God-given identity, character, status, belonging, or destiny, with debate over whether Revelation emphasizes personal identity, divine ownership, intimacy, or vindication.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies the new name with a renewed Word, covenant, and priesthood, privately known, received, and kept by its bearer.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"New name = transformed identity or status bestowed by God.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"New name = a renewed Word, covenant, and priesthood borne and privately apprehended by the recipient.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book uses the same principal renaming and Revelation texts but reclassifies what the name signifies: not chiefly the bearer’s transformed identity or status, but a renewed Word-covenant-priesthood complex. It also generalizes Revelation 2:17’s exclusive knowledge into the conditions of knowing, receiving, and keeping that complex.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The central symbolic referent is replaced. The consensus identifies the person’s divinely transformed identity/status; the book identifies renewed theological realities—Word, covenant, and priesthood—associated with the bearer. These may be related, but they are not the same referent.\",\"rationale\":\"The overlap in divine renewal, covenant setting, and reception by an overcomer does not make the cores equivalent. The book’s direct definition shifts the symbol from who the recipient becomes to what renewed realities the recipient bears. Because this replaces rather than merely narrows the consensus identification, the relationship is divergent rather than refined or novel. It is not novel because covenantal renewal and belonging are recognizable within the consensus field, even though the combined Word-covenant-priesthood identification is distinctive.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The book’s triad could be read as specifying the substantive contents or privileges of the new God-given identity, in which case it would be a refinement rather than a replacement. However, the entry grammatically identifies the new name with the triad itself, while the printed texts do not explicitly identify it with a renewed Word or priesthood.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
